    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's not inherently indicative of an unreliable model Just because the confidence interval crosses zero, it shows that there is uncertainty about the exact value of the effect, but it doesn't automatically make the model unreliable. In your methodology chapter, you can describe this as a case where statistical significance and confidence intervals provide mixed signals, necessitating cautious interpretation of your results.\n\nYou might phrase it like this:\n\n\n\"The statistical test resulted in a p-value of 0.042, suggesting evidence against the null hypothesis at the conventional 5% significance level. However, the 95% confidence interval, which ranges from a slight negative effect to a potential increase, overlaps zero, indicating that the true effect size could feasibly be zero. This juxtaposition highlights the nuanced nature of our findings and suggests that while the primary independent variable appears to have a significant impact, further investigation with a larger sample or refined analysis methods may help clarify the magnitude and direction of this effect, to ensure a more precise estimation of the relationship in question.\"\n\n\nConsider mentioning further research, potential confounders, or variables that might be causing this heterogeneity in the results. This will reflect an understanding of the limitations and show your subjects' thoroughness.",
