    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, discussing the \"noise\" in your data is a sensible approach to explaining these inconsistencies. In technical terms, \"noise\" refers to random error inherent to the data collection, measurement, or variable estimation process—a factor that can cause your estimates of the true effect to deviate from the population parameter.\n\nAn example to include in your thesis might be:\n\n\n\"The presence of 'noise' in data, a form of random error, is an inevitable feature that emerges during data collection and analysis, manifesting as variability or discrepancies not attributable to true signal or underlying relationships within the data set. Such random fluctuations can arise from measurement errors, sampling variability, or unobserved confounders, producing mixed results as seen in our study. These inconsistencies in the primary independent variable's estimates, despite achieving statistical significance, underscore the significance of 'noise' in research where even with meticulous methodologies, residual error can obscure clear causal interpretations.\"\n\n\nIn this way, you acknowledge the complexities of data gathering and introduce an advanced understanding of why such observed inconsistencies occur, thereby enhancing the technical depth and context of your explanation.",
